WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:06.240
When you are working in Chimera, you will usually&nbsp;
be visualizing protein structures. However, it is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:11.280
also possible to visualize structures containing&nbsp;
both protein and nucleic acid components.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:18.640
For example, here we have the PDB entry 1hys,&nbsp;
a reverse transcriptase enzyme from HIV.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:23.200
To change the depiction of the&nbsp;
nucleotides that the enzyme is holding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:30.320
we can go to Actions, Atoms and Bonds, and&nbsp;
Nucleotide Objects. Under Settings we see a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:36.480
panel containing the various depiction options.&nbsp;
For example, under the side chain section,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:45.040
we can change the Fill/Slab to Ladder. Now the&nbsp;
slabs are replaced with rods as shown on screen.

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:52.400
Chimera contains many other useful depiction&nbsp;
options under the Tools menu. For example,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:57.840
under Depiction, we can color the structure&nbsp;
according to a rainbow color scheme. I'm using&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.840 --> 00:01:03.440
the default color range as shown here, but you&nbsp;
can customize your own colors in the color editor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:10.080
When we hit Apply, the structure is colored&nbsp;
according to the range set above, starting with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:16.960
blue at each N-terminus and ending with red at&nbsp;
each C-terminus. Because this protein contains two&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.960 --> 00:01:22.880
chains, there are a total of four termini in the&nbsp;
structure, two of which are highlighted on screen.

00:01:24.880 --> 00:01:31.760
Under Tools and Depiction, we can also color&nbsp;
by secondary structure. If we apply the default&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.760 --> 00:01:38.960
settings, alpha helices are colored red, beta&nbsp;
sheets purple, and coils gray. Notice that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.960 --> 00:01:44.400
this coloration only applies to the protein&nbsp;
part of the structure, not the nucleic acids.

00:01:46.720 --> 00:01:51.840
Here I have recolored the structure to a more&nbsp;
uniform color scheme to demonstrate some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:59.120
advanced effects that Chimera has to offer. Under&nbsp;
Actions and Ribbon, we can activate Ribbon Edging.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.920 --> 00:02:05.920
This gives the ribbons thicker and more clearly&nbsp;
defined edges. For more effect options, we can&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.920 --> 00:02:13.120
go to Tools, Viewing Controls, and open up the&nbsp;
Effects panel. Among the variety of effects shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.120 --> 00:02:19.760
here is Depth Cueing. If we turn off Depth Cueing,&nbsp;
the structure takes on a much flatter appearance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.320 --> 00:02:24.960
You can also activate Silhouettes, which&nbsp;
give the structure a darker outline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.680 --> 00:02:31.920
You can adjust parameters such as width and&nbsp;
color. For the most clearly defined outlines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:38.320
set the transparency under A all the way&nbsp;
up to 1 and change the color to black.

00:02:40.560 --> 00:02:44.880
Finally, you can turn on Shadows for&nbsp;
a more three-dimensional appearance.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:52.000
Another interesting feature to explore&nbsp;
is the Side View. Using this feature,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:56.400
you can slice through the structure to&nbsp;
determine how much of it you will see.

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.920
You can also use Chimera to emulate the&nbsp;
distinctive art style that David Goodsell&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.920 --> 00:03:10.080
uses in his "Molecule of the Month" column&nbsp;
on PDB101. In the examples shown on screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:15.600
we can see that this style is characterized by&nbsp;
flat lighting and the use of individual atoms&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.440
as opposed to ribbons. To achieve this&nbsp;
style, the first step is to go to Tools,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.400
Viewing Controls, and Lighting. Here, we can&nbsp;
change the lighting from its default setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.400 --> 00:03:34.560
to Ambient. This creates the distinctive&nbsp;
flat look. With the lighting configured,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.560 --> 00:03:40.400
we can now go to Actions and show the&nbsp;
atoms and bonds. Then we must hide the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:49.120
protein ribbons by setting the selection&nbsp;
mode to Append, selecting chains A and B,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:58.640
and under Actions, choosing Ribbon Hide. With the&nbsp;
selection cleared, we can change the atoms and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:05.600
bonds depiction to Sphere. The result bears some&nbsp;
resemblance to the art style of David Goodsell.

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:13.840
You can take this one step further by going&nbsp;
to Actions, Color, and choosing By Element.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:22.800
Now each atom in the protein is colored according&nbsp;
to the standard elemental coloration scheme.

